# 0x2764

Portable dotfiles managed as [GNU Stow](https://www.gnu.org/software/stow/) packages, plus a safe importer for an existing home directory.

Each top-level package mirrors its destination relative to `$HOME`:

```text
0x2764/
├── hypr/.config/hypr/...
├── git/.gitconfig
├── ssh/.ssh/config
└── 0x2764-cli/.local/bin/0x2764
```

The package names in [`.0x2764-packages`](.0x2764-packages) are installed by default. Keeping an explicit manifest prevents documentation or future support directories from accidentally being treated as packages.

## Requirements

- Bash 4 or newer
- GNU Stow
- `fzf` only for the interactive config picker
- GNU `find`, `readlink`, and `realpath`

On Arch Linux:

```sh
sudo pacman -S stow fzf
```

## Bootstrap a clone

Clone the repository at any location, then run:

```sh
./bootstrap --dry-run  # inspect
./bootstrap            # stow every registered package into $HOME
```

The included `0x2764-cli` package installs `0x2764` into `~/.local/bin`, so the repository bootstraps its own command. Ensure that directory is on `PATH`, or continue invoking `./0x2764` from the clone.

Bootstrap is idempotent. GNU Stow will report a conflict rather than overwrite a real file.

## Import existing XDG configs

Run the interactive picker:

```sh
./0x2764 import
```

Or name directories explicitly:

```sh
./0x2764 import --dry-run hypr nvim waybar
./0x2764 import hypr nvim waybar
```

Use `--all` only after carefully inspecting the dry run:

```sh
./0x2764 import --all --dry-run
./0x2764 import --all --yes
```

Selected directories are moved from `${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}` into separate packages and immediately stowed back into place. For example:

```text
~/.config/hypr/             ->  0x2764/hypr/.config/hypr/
~/.config/hypr (symlink)    <-  created by Stow
```

The importer:

- never imports unselected directories;
- rejects symlinks, existing packages, and paths outside `$HOME`;
- simulates Stow after moving but before creating links;
- rolls the move back if simulation or Stow fails;
- asks before changing anything unless `--yes` is supplied;
- registers each successful package in `.0x2764-packages`.

Imports are intentionally performed one package at a time. If a later package in a batch conflicts, earlier successful packages remain imported and the failing package is rolled back.

> **Audit before importing.** Config trees can contain credentials, tokens, browser profiles, generated state, and machine-specific data. The tool cannot decide what is safe to commit.

If `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is customized, it must still be below `$HOME`, because this repository uses `$HOME` as Stow's common target. `--config-dir DIR` can override it for one import.

## Import other home files

`add` creates one new package while preserving every source path relative to `$HOME`:

```sh
./0x2764 add --dry-run git ~/.gitconfig
./0x2764 add git ~/.gitconfig

./0x2764 add zsh ~/.zshrc ~/.zprofile
./0x2764 add ssh ~/.ssh/config
```

A package must be new. This restriction makes rollback predictable and avoids modifying an already-stowed package during a failed import.

## Day-to-day commands

```sh
0x2764 list
0x2764 stow --dry-run          # all registered packages
0x2764 stow hypr nvim          # selected packages
0x2764 unstow hypr
0x2764 help
```

To add a package manually, create its Stow tree at the repository root and add its name to `.0x2764-packages`. Do not run Stow over the repository root itself; always pass explicit package names (the command does this for you).
